          "title": "Un e una",
          "body": "**A / an** is **un** (masculine) or **una** (feminine).\n\n| | maschile | femminile |\n|---|---|---|\n| before a consonant | ==un== cornetto | ==una== pizza |\n| before a vowel | ==un== amico | ==un'==amica |\n\n> Feminine words keep the a before a vowel: **un'**amica, not *un amica*.",
          "examples": [
            {
              "it": "Un caffè e una pizza.",
              "en": "A coffee and a pizza."
            },
            {
              "it": "Un amico e un'amica.",
              "en": "A (male) friend and a (female) friend."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "grammar_note",
          "id": "a1-02-l2-e03",
          "stage": "learn",
          "title": "Uno",
          "body": "Masculine words that start with **s + consonant**, **z** or **gn** take **uno**.\n\n| un | uno |\n|---|---|\n| ==un== gelato | ==uno== studente |\n| ==un== libro | ==uno== zaino |\n| ==un== caffè | ==uno== spritz |\n\n> **Uno** is only for masculine words. Feminine words always take **una**: una pizza.",
          "examples": [
            {
              "it": "Uno studente e uno zaino.",
              "en": "A student and a backpack."
            },
            {
              "it": "Uno spritz e un caffè.",
              "en": "A spritz and a coffee."
